## Values to put in

Each of these builds the value *and* the text for it, since a value that was not
read from a file has no text of its own yet. They are
[`Toml.Literal`](Toml-Literal)'s, offered here so that an edit needs one import
rather than two, and shared with [`Toml.Encode`](Toml-Encode) so that the two
cannot disagree about how to spell a float.

@docs string, multilineString, literalString, multilineLiteralString
@docs int, bigInt, float, bigDecimal, bool
@docs offsetDateTime, localDateTime, localDate, localTime
@docs array, inlineTable

-}

import Array exposing (Array)
import Basics exposing (..)
import BigDecimal exposing (BigDecimal)
import BigInt exposing (BigInt)
import Civil.Date as Date exposing (Date)
import Civil.DateTime as DateTime exposing (DateTime)
import Civil.Time as CivilTime exposing (Time)
import Maybe exposing (Maybe(..))
import String
import Toml.Ast as Ast exposing
    ( ArrayElement
    , ArrayLit
    , Comment
    , Content(..)
    , Document
    , Expression
    , Key
    , Newline(..)
    , SimpleKey
    , TableKind(..)
    , Trivia
    )
import Toml.Literal as Literal


{-| A value on its way into a document, built by one of the functions at the
bottom of this module.

The same type as [`Toml.Ast.Value`](Toml-Ast#Value), named here so that an edit
needs one import rather than two. The constructors are in `Toml.Ast`, for
reading a value apart; nothing here needs them, because a value is built by
[`string`](#string), [`int`](#int) and the rest.

-}
type alias Value =
    Ast.Value


{-| The comments that belong to one key.

    { blankBefore = True
    , leading = [ " How long to wait." ]
    , trailing = Just " seconds"
    }

    -->  <a blank line>
    -->  # How long to wait.
    -->  timeout = 30 # seconds

`leading` and `trailing` are the text *after* the `#`, the leading space
included, because `# note` and `#note` are different files and neither is this
module's to choose.

`blankBefore` is whether an empty line sits above the block. It is here rather
than left to whoever is writing the file because there is no other way to ask
for one -- a document has no lines to insert, only expressions -- and because a
comment block hard against the line above it reads as a continuation of that
line. It is always `False` at the very top of a document, where a file does not
begin with a blank line, and for the first key directly under its `[header]`,
which belongs against the header rather than held off it.

-}
type alias Comments =
    { blankBefore : Bool
    , leading : Array String
    , trailing : Maybe String
    }


-- LOOKING AROUND


{-| The value at a path, if a key there holds one.
-}
get : Array String -> Document -> Maybe Value
get path document =
    indexOf path document
        |> Maybe.andThen
            (\index ->
                when Array.get index document.expressions is
                    Just expression ->
                        when expression.content is
                            Pair fields ->
                                Just fields.keyval.value

                            _ ->
                                Nothing

                    Nothing ->
                        Nothing
            )


{-| Whether a key is in the document at all.

[`get`](#get) answers this too, but answering it with `get` means holding a
`Toml.Ast.Value` in order to throw it away, and the question a program writing
a config file asks is usually this one: **is this key already here, or am I the
one putting it there?** That is what decides whether it needs an explanation
written above it -- see [`introduce`](#introduce).

-}
member : Array String -> Document -> Bool
member path document =
    indexOf path document /= Nothing


{-| Every key in the document, in the order the file writes them.

Table headers are not keys and are not here; the path of a key written under
`[server]` begins with `server` all the same.

-}
paths : Document -> Array (Array String)
paths document =
    Array.map2
        (\expression header ->
            when expression.content is
                Pair fields ->
                    Just (Array.prepend header (Literal.path fields.keyval.key))

                _ ->
                    Nothing
        )
        document.expressions
        (headers document.expressions)
        |> Array.mapAndKeepJust identity


{-| The comments that belong to a key, by the convention above.

    { blankBefore = True
    , leading = [ " how long to wait" ]
    , trailing = Just " seconds"
    }

This and [`setComments`](#setComments) are inverses: what one reads, the other
writes back unchanged. A key that is not in the document has no comments and
reads as `{ blankBefore = False, leading = [], trailing = Nothing }`.

-}
comments : Array String -> Document -> Comments
comments path document =
    when indexOf path document is
        Nothing ->
            noComments

        Just index ->
            commentsAt index document


{-| The comments that belong to a table's `[header]` line: the block directly
above it and the comment beside it, by the same convention as a key's, and the
same ones [`removeTable`](#removeTable) takes with the table.

    Toml.Edit.tableComments [ "servers", "alpha" ]

    -->  { blankBefore = True, leading = [ " The primary." ], trailing = Nothing }

For an array of tables the path means the **first** `[[header]]`, which is the
other way round from every other path in this module. A path into an array of
tables names its last item when it names a *value*, because that is the item
TOML means by it. A comment over a header explains the key -- the whole array --
and the first header is where [`Toml.Encode`](Toml-Encode#commented) writes it.
The other items are reached through the AST.

A table with no header line of its own -- one that only `[a.b]` implied, or one
a dotted key built -- has no line for a comment to be on, and reads as
`{ blankBefore = False, leading = [], trailing = Nothing }`. So does a table that
is not there.

-}
tableComments : Array String -> Document -> Comments
tableComments path document =
    when headerIndexOf path document is
        Nothing ->
            noComments

        Just index ->
            commentsAt index document


noComments : Comments
noComments =
    { blankBefore = False, leading = [], trailing = Nothing }


{-| The comments owned by the expression at `index`, a key or a header.
-}
commentsAt : Int -> Document -> Comments
commentsAt index document =
    let
        start =
            firstOwned index document.expressions
    in
    { blankBefore = isBlank (start - 1) document.expressions
    , leading =
        Array.slice start index document.expressions
            |> Array.mapAndKeepJust ownComment
    , trailing =
        Array.get index document.expressions
            |> Maybe.andThen trailingCommentOf
    }


trailingCommentOf : Expression -> Maybe String
trailingCommentOf expression =
    when expression.content is
        Pair fields ->
            Maybe.map .text fields.comment

        Header fields ->
            Maybe.map .text fields.comment

        Empty _ ->
            Nothing


ownComment : Expression -> Maybe String
ownComment expression =
    when expression.content is
        Empty fields ->
            Maybe.map .text fields.comment

        _ ->
            Nothing


isHeader : Int -> Array Expression -> Bool
isHeader index expressions =
    when Maybe.map .content (Array.get index expressions) is
        Just (Header _) ->
            True

        _ ->
            False


{-| Whether the expression at `index` is an empty line with no comment on it.

`False` for an index off either end, which is what makes the top of the file
answer "no blank line above": there is nothing above it to be blank. The guard
on a negative index is not defensive -- `Array.get -1` in Gren is the *last*
element, and without it the first key of a file was asked whether the line
after the final newline was blank, and it was.

-}
isBlank : Int -> Array Expression -> Bool
isBlank index expressions =
    when
        (if index < 0 then
            Nothing

         else
            Array.get index expressions
        )
    is
        Just expression ->
            when expression.content is
                Empty fields ->
                    fields.comment == Nothing

                _ ->
                    False

        Nothing ->
            False


{-| Set the comments that belong to a key, by the same convention
[`comments`](#comments) reads them by.

    Toml.Edit.setComments [ "timeout" ]
        { blankBefore = True
        , leading = [ " How long to wait." ]
        , trailing = Just " seconds"
        }

    -->  <a blank line>
    -->  # How long to wait.
    -->  timeout = 30 # seconds

`comments` and this are inverses, so what one reads the other writes back
unchanged -- including the blank line, which is why [`Comments`](#Comments) has
a field for it.

`leading = []` takes the block above the key away and `trailing = Nothing` takes
the comment off the line. Anything separated from the key by a blank line is not
the key's and is left alone, which is the same blank line that protects it from
[`remove`](#remove). A key that is not there is left alone too.

`blankBefore = True` puts a blank line above the block if there is not one
already, and `False` takes one away if there is. Asking for one does nothing at
the top of the document, where there is no line above to be blank, and does
nothing directly under a `[header]`, where the key is the first of its table and
a blank would hold it off the header it belongs to -- the same rule
[`Toml.Encode`](Toml-Encode) writes by. Taking one away is the
one thing here that reaches past the key's own lines, so it is worth knowing
what it does: the blank line is what separates a floating comment from an owned
one, and removing it makes the block above join this key's -- which is to say
it will go when this key goes.

-}
setComments : Array String -> Comments -> Document -> Document
setComments path wanted document =
    when indexOf path document is
        Nothing ->
            document

        Just index ->
            setCommentsAt AKey index wanted document


{-| Set the comments that belong to a table's `[header]` line, by the same
convention [`tableComments`](#tableComments) reads them by.

    Toml.Edit.setTableComments [ "servers", "alpha" ]
        { blankBefore = True, leading = [ " The primary." ], trailing = Nothing }

    -->  <a blank line>
    -->  # The primary.
    -->  [servers.alpha]

The two are inverses, as [`comments`](#comments) and
[`setComments`](#setComments) are, and for an array of tables the path means
the first `[[header]]` for the reason given there.

This is how a program explains a section it created. [`introduce`](#introduce)
explains a key, and the header it brought with that key has nothing over it
until this is called; calling it only when [`member`](#member) said the first
key was not there yet keeps the explanation to the run that invented the
section, which is the same rule `introduce` keeps for a key.

One difference from `setComments`: `blankBefore = True` directly under another
header *is* honoured. The first key of a table belongs against its header, but a
nested header directly under its parent is two sections meeting, and the blank
between them is the one [`Toml.Encode`](Toml-Encode) keeps. At the top of the
file it is still a no-op.

A table with no header line, or none at all, is left alone.

-}
setTableComments : Array String -> Comments -> Document -> Document
setTableComments path wanted document =
    when headerIndexOf path document is
        Nothing ->
            document

        Just index ->
            setCommentsAt ATable index wanted document


{-| What a comment block is being written above. A key directly under its
`[header]` is not held off it by a blank line; a nested header directly under
its parent is two sections meeting, and may be.
-}
type Owner
    = AKey
    | ATable


setCommentsAt : Owner -> Int -> Comments -> Document -> Document
setCommentsAt owner index wanted document =
    let
        indent =
            Array.get index document.expressions
                |> Maybe.map .indent
                |> Maybe.withDefault ""

        block =
            Array.map
                (\text ->
                    { indent = indent
                    , content = Empty { comment = Just { text = text } }
                    , newline = Just (styleOf document)
                    }
                )
                wanted.leading

        start =
            firstOwned index document.expressions
    in
    rebuild document
        (Array.prepend
            (Array.prepend
                (spacedTo owner
                    wanted.blankBefore
                    (styleOf document)
                    (Array.takeFirst start document.expressions)
                )
                block
            )
            (Array.dropFirst index document.expressions
                |> Array.update 0 (setTrailingComment wanted.trailing)
            )
        )


{-| Everything above a comment block, with a blank line at the end of it or
without one, as asked.

A blank is not added in two places. At the top of the document there is no
line above to be blank. Directly under a `[header]`, when what is being
explained is a key, there is one, but the key is the first of its table and a
blank there would push it away from the header it belongs to -- the same rule
[`Toml.Encode`](Toml-Encode) writes by, and the case [`introduce`](#introduce)
lands in every time it is the call that brought the header with it. A nested
header under its parent is the other way: two sections meeting, and the blank
is wanted. Taking a blank away works everywhere.

-}
spacedTo : Owner -> Bool -> Newline -> Array Expression -> Array Expression
spacedTo owner wanted newline before =
    when { wanted = wanted, has = isBlank (Array.length before - 1) before } is
        { wanted = True, has = False } ->
            if Array.length before == 0 || (owner == AKey && isHeader (Array.length before - 1) before) then
                before

            else
                Array.pushLast
                    { indent = "", content = Empty { comment = Nothing }, newline = Just newline }
                    before

        { wanted = False, has = True } ->
            Array.dropLast 1 before

        _ ->
            before


{-| The comment on the key's own line, or on the header's.

The whitespace before it is the line's and is kept, except that a comment being
added to a line that had none gets a space put in front of it -- `x = 1# note`
is legal and nobody writes it -- and a comment being taken off takes that
whitespace with it, rather than leaving the line ending in spaces.

-}
setTrailingComment : Maybe String -> Expression -> Expression
setTrailingComment text expression =
    let
        spaced current =
            when text is
                Just _ ->
                    if current == "" then
                        " "

                    else
                        current

                Nothing ->
                    ""

        comment =
            Maybe.map (\it -> { text = it }) text
    in
    when expression.content is
        Pair fields ->
            { expression | content = Pair { fields | trailing = spaced fields.trailing, comment = comment } }

        Header fields ->
            { expression | content = Header { fields | trailing = spaced fields.trailing, comment = comment } }

        Empty _ ->
            expression


-- CHANGING


{-| Put a value at a path.

If a key is already there, only its value changes: the key keeps its spelling,
the equals sign keeps the whitespace around it, and the comment on the line stays
where it was. Nothing else in the file moves.

If there is no such key, one is added. It goes at the end of the table it belongs
to, after the last key already in it, indented to match. If the table does not
exist either, a `[header]` for it is appended to the end of the file with the key
beneath and a blank line above, and a file that ended with a newline still does.

A top-level key added to a file that has no top-level keys yet goes *before* the
first `[header]`, with a blank line between, since anything written after a
header belongs to that header's table. It goes above the comment block directly
over that header too, because that block is the header's by the convention
above; a comment with a blank line under it stays where it is.

**A value that is already what you are setting it to is left exactly as it is
written.** This is not thrift, it is the difference between an editor and an
encoder, and it is the one thing about this module worth reading twice.

Setting a value replaces the whitespace inside it, because the whitespace was
part of the old value and the new one has none of its own. So a list somebody
arranged by hand:

    ports = [
      8000,  # http
      8001   # https
    ]

written back with `set [ "ports" ] (array [ int 8000, int 8001 ])` would come
out as `ports = [8000, 8001]`. That is right for a list that *changed* -- there
is no old formatting to keep for a new value -- and it is destructive for one
that did not.

Which is the case that actually happens. A program that writes its whole
configuration out on every save touches every key, so the key the user
hand-arranged gets flattened by a save about some *other* key. Comparing first
is what stops that, and doing it here rather than leaving it to every caller is
the only way it gets done: the caller would have to hold the old document, take
it apart, and know that `1.50` and `1.5` are the same number and `0x1F` and
`31` are the same integer.

The comparison is by value and not by text. A string is its characters after
escapes, an integer or float is its number whatever base or spelling it was
written in, a date-time is the instant and the offset, an array is its elements
in order, and an inline table is its entries in order. So `set` over `0x1F` with
`int 31` changes nothing and the file keeps saying `0x1F`. To change the
spelling rather than the value, there is [`respell`](#respell).

An edit that gives the file a meaning it cannot have -- a key under an inline
table, say -- is not refused here. See [what this module does not
check](#what-this-module-does-not-check).

-}
set : Array String -> Value -> Document -> Document
set path content document =
    when indexOf path document is
        Just index ->
            if Maybe.map (sameValue content) (get path document) == Just True then
                document

            else
                rebuild document
                    (Array.update index (replaceValue content) document.expressions)

        Nothing ->
            insert path content document


{-| Write a value whether or not the file already means it, so that the
*spelling* changes and not only the value.

    Toml.Edit.respell [ "server", "port" ] (Toml.Edit.int 31)

    -->  port = 31          # was: port = 0x1F

[`set`](#set) with its one rule taken away. That rule -- leave a value alone
that already says what it is being set to -- is what makes writing a whole
configuration back safe on every save, and it is also what stops a program from
ever changing how a value is written. This is the door for the second thing,
and it is a separate door on purpose: a program that opens it does so on the
one key it means to, rather than for every key it saves.

The key keeps everything but its value: the comments that belong to it, the
whitespace around the equals sign, and its place in the file. That is the
difference between this and [`remove`](#remove) followed by `set`, which was
the only way to do it before and takes the key's comments away with the line.

What it is for, in practice, is a form a program has changed its mind about --
a value it used to write with [`string`](#string) and now writes with
[`multilineString`](#multilineString), where `set` would leave every file
written by the older version spelled the old way. Reach for it there, and not
in the code that saves everything: writing a spelling unconditionally on every
save is how a user's own `'C:\Users'` gets turned back into a basic string,
over and over, with nothing they can do to make it stick.

On a key that is not in the document this is exactly `set`, since a value with
no spelling in the file yet cannot be spelled differently.

-}
respell : Array String -> Value -> Document -> Document
respell path content document =
    when indexOf path document is
        Just index ->
            rebuild document
                (Array.update index (replaceValue content) document.expressions)

        Nothing ->
            insert path content document


{-| Set a value, and explain the key if this is the call that put it there.

    Toml.Edit.introduce [ "theme" ]
        { blankBefore = True
        , leading = [ " Which colour scheme to open in." ]
        , trailing = Nothing
        }
        (Toml.Edit.string "dark")

[`set`](#set) and [`setComments`](#setComments) together, except that the
comments are written **only when the key was not already in the document**.

That condition is the whole reason this exists, and it is a rule about the
person on the other end of the file rather than about TOML. A program that
keeps a config file wants every key it invents to arrive with a sentence saying
what the key is for, because a config file whose fields are undocumented is one
nobody opens. It does not want to write that sentence again on every save: the
user who deleted it meant to delete it, and the user who rewrote it in their own
language has not made a mistake for the next save to correct.

On a document with the key already in it this is exactly `set`, comments and
all left alone. On [`Toml.empty`](Toml#empty) it writes the key and the
explanation together, which is how a file that has never existed comes out
documented.

-}
introduce : Array String -> Comments -> Value -> Document -> Document
introduce path note content document =
    if member path document then
        set path content document

    else
        set path content document
            |> setComments path note


{-| Add one element to the end of the array a key holds, leaving the elements
already in it exactly as they are written.

    Toml.Edit.appendTo [ "zones" ] (Toml.Edit.string "Europe/Oslo")

    -->  zones = [
    -->    "Asia/Seoul",      # them
    -->    "America/Chicago", # me
    -->    "Europe/Oslo",
    -->  ]

[`set`](#set) can write a whole array, and that is the right thing for a list
whose every element is new. It is the wrong thing for a list a person has
arranged over four lines with a note against each one, because the new array has
no formatting and the old formatting is what gets thrown away. These three
functions are the other half: they change one element and leave the file to say
everything else the way it said it.

The new element copies the shape of the boundary in front of it -- the newline
and the indent if the array has them, a space after the comma if it is on one
line -- and takes the trailing comma the last element had, or leaves it off if
that one did not have one.

A key that is not there yet is written as a one-element array, which is what
makes "add this to my list" work on a file that does not exist. A key holding
something that is not an array is left alone.

-}
appendTo : Array String -> Value -> Document -> Document
appendTo path content document =
    when get path document is
        Just (Ast.ArrayVal lit) ->
            setArray path (appended content lit) document

        Just _ ->
            document

        Nothing ->
            set path (array [ content ]) document


{-| Replace one element of the array a key holds, by position, and touch nothing
else -- not the elements around it, not the whitespace, not the notes.

    Toml.Edit.setAt [ "zones" ] 1 (Toml.Edit.string "America/Denver")

    -->  zones = [
    -->    "Asia/Seoul",     # them
    -->    "America/Denver", # me
    -->  ]

An index that is not in the array leaves the document alone, and so does a key
that is missing or is not an array. As with [`set`](#set), an element that
already means what it is being set to is not rewritten: setting it would replace
the whitespace *inside* it, and a program that writes its whole configuration
back on every save would otherwise flatten an array it was not changing. To
write the spelling anyway there is [`respellAt`](#respellAt).

-}
setAt : Array String -> Int -> Value -> Document -> Document
setAt path index content document =
    when elementOf path index document is
        Just found ->
            if sameValue content found.element.value then
                document

            else
                setArray path (withValueAt index content found.lit) document

        Nothing ->
            document


{-| Replace one element of an array whether or not it already means what it is
being set to, so that the *spelling* of that element changes.

    Toml.Edit.respellAt [ "zones" ] 1
        (Maybe.withDefault (Toml.Edit.string "America/Chicago")
            (Toml.Edit.literalString "America/Chicago")
        )

    -->  zones = [
    -->    "Asia/Seoul",      # them
    -->    'America/Chicago', # me
    -->  ]

[`setAt`](#setAt) is to this what [`set`](#set) is to [`respell`](#respell), and
for the same reason: the comparison that leaves an unchanged value alone is
worth keeping on by default, and changing how one element is written is the one
thing it cannot let you do. The element keeps its line, its indent and the note
written against it; only the value between them is rewritten.

Unlike `respell` there is no falling back to writing the key, since a position
in an array that is not there is not a place a value can go. An index out of
range, a missing key or a key that is not an array all leave the document alone.

-}
respellAt : Array String -> Int -> Value -> Document -> Document
respellAt path index content document =
    when elementOf path index document is
        Just found ->
            setArray path (withValueAt index content found.lit) document

        Nothing ->
            document


{-| The array a key holds and one element of it, which is what both of those
have to find before they can do anything.
-}
elementOf :
    Array String
    -> Int
    -> Document
    -> Maybe { lit : ArrayLit, element : ArrayElement }
elementOf path index document =
    when get path document is
        Just (Ast.ArrayVal lit) ->
            elementAt index lit
                |> Maybe.map (\element -> { lit = lit, element = element })

        _ ->
            Nothing


{-| The array with one element holding a different value and everything else
about that element -- its line, its indent, its comma -- as it was.
-}
withValueAt : Int -> Value -> ArrayLit -> ArrayLit
withValueAt index content lit =
    { lit | elements = Array.update index (\element -> { element | value = content }) lit.elements }


{-| Take one element out of the array a key holds, by position, with the note
that belongs to it.

    Toml.Edit.removeAt [ "zones" ] 0

    -->  zones = [
    -->    "America/Chicago", # me
    -->  ]

Which note belongs to which element is a convention, the same way it is for a
key, and this is it: **the comment after an element's comma, up to the end of
that line, is that element's.** It is the one a person writes against the value,
and it goes when the value goes. Everything after that line ending is the next
element's indent and stays where it is.

The last element is the one to watch, because the two things that make the array
close -- the trailing comma and where the `]` sits -- are written against it. The
element before it inherits both.

An index that is not in the array leaves the document alone, and so does a key
that is missing or is not an array. A negative one is not a position from the
end here; it is out of range like any other.

-}
removeAt : Array String -> Int -> Document -> Document
removeAt path index document =
    when get path document is
        Just (Ast.ArrayVal lit) ->
            when removedFrom index lit is
                Just shorter ->
                    setArray path shorter document

                Nothing ->
                    document

        _ ->
            document


{-| Put an array back where it came from. The value is known to have changed --
the three above have all looked at it already -- so this does not ask
[`sameValue`](#sameValue) a second time.
-}
setArray : Array String -> ArrayLit -> Document -> Document
setArray path lit document =
    when indexOf path document is
        Just index ->
            rebuild document
                (Array.update index (replaceValue (Ast.ArrayVal lit)) document.expressions)

        Nothing ->
            document


{-| The element at an index, if there is one. `Array.get -1` in Gren is the last
element, and that is not what an index out of range should mean here.
-}
elementAt : Int -> ArrayLit -> Maybe ArrayElement
elementAt index lit =
    if index < 0 then
        Nothing

    else
        Array.get index lit.elements


{-| The array with one more element on the end.

The new element's `before` is the trivia that used to sit before the closing
bracket -- which is where the old last element's note lives -- followed by that
element's own indent, so the note stays on its line and the new value starts
where the one above it did. What is left in front of the bracket is the same
trivia with the comments taken out of it, since a comment written against a
value must not be left behind pointing at nothing.

The old last element's `after` moves to the new one with it. Where a comma was
about to be written there is no longer the place for the space that was holding
the closing bracket off, and `[ 1, 2 ]` would otherwise gain one at `2 ,`.

-}
appended : Value -> ArrayLit -> ArrayLit
appended content lit =
    when Array.popLast lit.elements is
        Nothing ->
            { elements = [ { before = lit.trailing, value = content, after = [], comma = False } ]
            , trailing = withoutComments lit.trailing
            }

        Just { last, initial } ->
            { elements =
                initial
                    |> Array.pushLast { last | comma = True, after = [] }
                    |> Array.pushLast
                        { before =
                            if Array.isEmpty lit.trailing then
                                last.before

                            else
                                Array.prepend lit.trailing (indentOf last.before)
                        , value = content
                        , after = last.after
                        , comma = last.comma
                        }
            , trailing = withoutComments lit.trailing
            }


{-| The array with one element gone, and the boundaries either side of it joined
back up.

For anything but the last element, what survives is the removed element's own
`before` up to the end of the line above it -- which carries the *previous*
element's note -- followed by the next element's indent. On one line, where
there is no line ending in either, that is just the separator that was in front
of the element, which is what a person would have left there.

The last element is the special one. Its note is written after it, so what
survives goes in front of the closing bracket, and only when that bracket is on
a line of its own; an array that ends `2 ]` has nothing there to keep. The
element before it takes over its comma, and its `after` as well, which is where
the space in `[ 1, 2 ]` that holds the bracket off is kept.

-}
removedFrom : Int -> ArrayLit -> Maybe ArrayLit
removedFrom index lit =
    when elementAt index lit is
        Nothing ->
            Nothing

        Just gone ->
            if index == Array.length lit.elements - 1 then
                Just
                    { elements =
                        Array.dropLast 1 lit.elements
                            |> Array.update
                                (index - 1)
                                (\previous ->
                                    { previous
                                        | comma = gone.comma
                                        , after =
                                            if Array.isEmpty gone.after then
                                                previous.after

                                            else
                                                gone.after
                                    }
                                )
                    , trailing =
                        if hasBreak lit.trailing then
                            Array.prepend (upToFirstBreak gone.before) (afterFirstBreak lit.trailing)

                        else
                            lit.trailing
                    }

            else
                Just
                    { lit
                        | elements =
                            Array.remove index lit.elements
                                |> Array.update
                                    index
                                    (\next ->
                                        { next
                                            | before =
                                                Array.prepend (upToFirstBreak gone.before)
                                                    (afterFirstBreak next.before)
                                        }
                                    )
                    }


{-| The pieces of a boundary up to and including the end of the line it starts
on -- the part that belongs to whatever was written above it.
-}
upToFirstBreak : Trivia -> Trivia
upToFirstBreak pieces =
    when firstBreak pieces is
        Just at ->
            Array.takeFirst (at + 1) pieces

        Nothing ->
            pieces


{-| The rest of it: the indent that belongs to whatever comes next.
-}
afterFirstBreak : Trivia -> Trivia
afterFirstBreak pieces =
    when firstBreak pieces is
        Just at ->
            Array.dropFirst (at + 1) pieces

        Nothing ->
            []


{-| The indent a line starts with, which is whatever follows its last line
ending.
-}
indentOf : Trivia -> Trivia
indentOf pieces =
    when lastBreak pieces is
        Just at ->
            Array.dropFirst (at + 1) pieces

        Nothing ->
            []


firstBreak : Trivia -> Maybe Int
firstBreak pieces =
    Array.indexedFoldl
        (\index piece found ->
            when { found = found, piece = piece } is
                { found = Nothing, piece = Ast.Break _ } ->
                    Just index

                _ ->
                    found
        )
        Nothing
        pieces


lastBreak : Trivia -> Maybe Int
lastBreak pieces =
    Array.indexedFoldl
        (\index piece found ->
            when piece is
                Ast.Break _ ->
                    Just index

                _ ->
                    found
        )
        Nothing
        pieces


hasBreak : Trivia -> Bool
hasBreak pieces =
    firstBreak pieces /= Nothing


{-| The same trivia with the comments taken out and the spaces that were holding
them off taken out with them, so that nothing is left pointing at a line that no
longer says anything.
-}
withoutComments : Trivia -> Trivia
withoutComments pieces =
    Array.foldl
        (\piece kept ->
            when piece is
                Ast.Break fields ->
                    if fields.comment == Nothing then
                        Array.pushLast piece kept

                    else
                        Array.pushLast (Ast.Break { fields | comment = Nothing })
                            (dropTrailingSpaces kept)

                _ ->
                    Array.pushLast piece kept
        )
        []
        pieces


dropTrailingSpaces : Trivia -> Trivia
dropTrailingSpaces pieces =
    when Array.last pieces is
        Just (Ast.Spaces _) ->
            Array.dropLast 1 pieces

        _ ->
            pieces


{-| Whether two values mean the same thing, whatever they were written as.

Formatting is not part of the answer, which is the point: this is what lets
[`set`](#set) leave a value alone that is already what it is being set to, and
formatting is precisely what would be lost if it did not. So `0x1F` equals `31`,
`1.50` equals `1.5`, `'a'` equals `"a"`, and an array equals one with the same
elements however the brackets were spaced.

Order counts inside an array, which is a fact about TOML, and inside an inline
table, which is not -- `{ a = 1, b = 2 }` and `{ b = 2, a = 1 }` mean the same
table and are not equal here. The looser answer would be the correct one and it
is not the useful one: a program that reorders an inline table wrote the entries
in the order it wants them, and this function exists to decide whether to leave
the file alone, not to settle what TOML means.

-}
sameValue : Value -> Value -> Bool
sameValue left right =
    when { left = left, right = right } is
        { left = Ast.StringVal a, right = Ast.StringVal b } ->
            a.value == b.value

        { left = Ast.IntegerVal a, right = Ast.IntegerVal b } ->
            BigInt.compare a.value b.value == EQ

        { left = Ast.FloatVal a, right = Ast.FloatVal b } ->
            sameFloat a.value b.value

        { left = Ast.BooleanVal a, right = Ast.BooleanVal b } ->
            a.value == b.value

        { left = Ast.OffsetDateTimeVal a, right = Ast.OffsetDateTimeVal b } ->
            a.value == b.value

        { left = Ast.LocalDateTimeVal a, right = Ast.LocalDateTimeVal b } ->
            a.value == b.value

        { left = Ast.LocalDateVal a, right = Ast.LocalDateVal b } ->
            a.value == b.value

        { left = Ast.LocalTimeVal a, right = Ast.LocalTimeVal b } ->
            a.value == b.value

        { left = Ast.ArrayVal a, right = Ast.ArrayVal b } ->
            sameSequence (Array.map .value a.elements) (Array.map .value b.elements)

        { left = Ast.InlineTableVal a, right = Ast.InlineTableVal b } ->
            (Array.length a.entries == Array.length b.entries)
                && (Array.map2
                        (\one other ->
                            Literal.path one.keyval.key == Literal.path other.keyval.key
                                && sameValue one.keyval.value other.keyval.value
                        )
                        a.entries
                        b.entries
                        |> Array.all identity
                   )

        _ ->
            False


{-| `BigDecimal.compare` for the finite ones, which is what makes `1.50` and
`1.5` equal. The other two are not numbers and compare as themselves, sign
included, since `+nan` and `-nan` are different text and TOML says nothing
about what the difference means.
-}
sameFloat : Ast.FloatValue -> Ast.FloatValue -> Bool
sameFloat left right =
    when { left = left, right = right } is
        { left = Ast.Finite a, right = Ast.Finite b } ->
            BigDecimal.compare a b == EQ

        { left = Ast.Infinite a, right = Ast.Infinite b } ->
            a == b

        { left = Ast.NotANumber a, right = Ast.NotANumber b } ->
            a == b

        _ ->
            False


sameSequence : Array Value -> Array Value -> Bool
sameSequence left right =
    Array.length left == Array.length right
        && (Array.map2 sameValue left right |> Array.all identity)


{-| Only the value. The key keeps its spelling, the equals sign keeps the
whitespace around it, and the comment on the line stays where it was.
-}
replaceValue : Value -> Expression -> Expression
replaceValue content expression =
    when expression.content is
        Pair fields ->
            let
                pair =
                    fields.keyval
            in
            { expression | content = Pair { fields | keyval = { pair | value = content } } }

        _ ->
            expression


{-| Take a key out, and the comments that belong to it with it.

The line goes, its leading comment block goes, and its trailing comment goes with
the line it was on. A floating comment -- one with a blank line above it -- stays,
which is the whole point of the blank line.

Nothing else changes. Removing the last key of a table leaves the `[header]`
behind, because an empty table is still a table and saying otherwise would be
this module deciding something the file did not.

-}
remove : Array String -> Document -> Document
remove path document =
    when indexOf path document is
        Nothing ->
            document

        Just index ->
            rebuild document
                (cut (firstOwned index document.expressions) (index + 1) document.expressions)


{-| Give a key a new name and change nothing else.

    Toml.Edit.rename [ "server", "port" ] "listen"

    -->  [server]
    -->  listen = 8443       # was: port = 8443

The value stays as it was written, the whitespace around the equals sign stays,
and the comments stay with the line. The new name is spelled bare where the
grammar allows it and quoted where it does not.

Only the last part of the path is the name: this renames the key `port`, not the
table `server`. For a table see [`renameTable`](#renameTable).

Whether the new name is already taken is not asked here. It is not a question
about syntax, so the answer comes from
[`Toml.Table.fromDocument`](Toml-Table#fromDocument), like every other question
of its kind -- see the two layers in [`Toml.Table`](Toml-Table).

-}
rename : Array String -> String -> Document -> Document
rename path name document =
    when indexOf path document is
        Nothing ->
            document

        Just index ->
            let
                at =
                    Array.length path - Array.length (headerAt index document) - 1
            in
            rebuild document
                (Array.update index (renameKeyPart at name) document.expressions)


{-| Give a table a new name: its own `[header]`, every header nested under it,
and every dotted key that spells the name out itself.

    Toml.Edit.renameTable [ "server" ] "listener"

    -->  [listener]
    -->  [listener.tls]

Only the last part of the path is the name, as with [`rename`](#rename), so a
table's ancestors never move and nothing has to be lifted into a different
section for the result to mean what it says.

A dotted key changes only where the renamed part is a table it passes *through*:
`renameTable [ "a" ] "x"` turns `a.b = 1` into `x.b = 1` and leaves `b` alone,
because `b` names the value rather than a table. By the same rule this does
nothing to a path that names a key: `[ "server", "port" ]` is not a table, and
[`rename`](#rename) is the one that renames it.

-}
renameTable : Array String -> String -> Document -> Document
renameTable path name document =
    let
        at =
            Array.length path - 1
    in
    rebuild document
        (Array.map2
            (\expression header ->
                when expression.content is
                    Header fields ->
                        if prefixOf path (Literal.path fields.header.key) then
                            renameHeaderPart at name expression

                        else
                            expression

                    Pair fields ->
                        let
                            own =
                                Literal.path fields.keyval.key

                            inKey =
                                at - Array.length header

                            -- Above zero, the renamed part is in the header
                            -- rather than in the key. At the end of the key it
                            -- names the value, and a value is not a table.
                            namesATable =
                                inKey >= 0 && inKey < Array.length own - 1
                        in
                        if namesATable && prefixOf path (Array.prepend header own) then
                            renameKeyPart inKey name expression

                        else
                            expression

                    Empty _ ->
                        expression
            )
            document.expressions
            (headers document.expressions)
        )


{-| The path of the table header in effect at an expression.
-}
headerAt : Int -> Document -> Array String
headerAt index document =
    Array.get index (headers document.expressions)
        |> Maybe.withDefault []


renameKeyPart : Int -> String -> Expression -> Expression
renameKeyPart at name expression =
    when expression.content is
        Pair fields ->
            let
                pair =
                    fields.keyval
            in
            { expression | content = Pair { fields | keyval = { pair | key = replacePart at name pair.key } } }

        _ ->
            expression


renameHeaderPart : Int -> String -> Expression -> Expression
renameHeaderPart at name expression =
    when expression.content is
        Header fields ->
            let
                head =
                    fields.header
            in
            { expression | content = Header { fields | header = { head | key = replacePart at name head.key } } }

        _ ->
            expression


{-| One part of a key, by its position along the dots. Out of range is left
alone, which is what makes the callers above able to say "the part at this
depth" without checking first.
-}
replacePart : Int -> String -> Key -> Key
replacePart at name whole =
    if at == 0 then
        { whole | first = Literal.simpleKey name }

    else
        { whole
            | rest =
                Array.update (at - 1)
                    (\part -> { part | key = Literal.simpleKey name })
                    whole.rest
        }


{-| Take a whole table out: its `[header]`, everything under it, and the comments
that belong to the header.

Sub-tables go too, since `[a.b]` is under `[a]`. An array of tables named by the
path goes entirely, every item of it.

-}
removeTable : Array String -> Document -> Document
removeTable path document =
    let
        owned =
            Array.map2 (under path)
                (headers document.expressions)
                document.expressions

        dropped =
            Array.indexedMap
                (\index isOwned ->
                    isOwned || leadsAnOwnedBlock index owned document.expressions
                )
                owned
    in
    rebuild document
        (Array.map2
            (\expression drop ->
                if drop then
                    Nothing

                else
                    Just expression
            )
            document.expressions
            dropped
            |> Array.mapAndKeepJust identity
        )


{-| Whether this expression is part of the comment block leading a table that is
being removed.

A table may appear in more than one place -- `[a]`, then `[c]`, then `[a.b]` --
so this looks for the start of every run of removed expressions rather than
assuming there is one.

-}
leadsAnOwnedBlock : Int -> Array Bool -> Array Expression -> Bool
leadsAnOwnedBlock index owned expressions =
    let
        startsHere at =
            Maybe.withDefault False (Array.get at owned)
                && not (Maybe.withDefault False (Array.get (at - 1) owned))

        insideCommentBlockOf at =
            startsHere at && firstOwned at expressions <= index
    in
    Array.indexedFoldl
        (\at _ found -> found || (at > index && insideCommentBlockOf at))
        False
        owned


{-| Whether an expression belongs to the table at `path`: the header itself, or
anything the header is in effect for, or any table nested under it.
-}
under : Array String -> Array String -> Expression -> Bool
under path header expression =
    when expression.content is
        Header fields ->
            prefixOf path (Literal.path fields.header.key)

        _ ->
            prefixOf path header


prefixOf : Array String -> Array String -> Bool
prefixOf path candidate =
    Array.length candidate >= Array.length path
        && Array.takeFirst (Array.length path) candidate == path


{-| Put an edited expression list back into its document, with the two
line-ending invariants restored.

Both of them are easy to break and invisible when broken until the file is
written: every expression but the last needs a newline, and whether the *last*
one has one is what says if the file ends with a newline. An edit that removes
the final line, or adds one after it, changes that without meaning to.

-}
rebuild : Document -> Array Expression -> Document
rebuild original expressions =
    { original
        | expressions =
            expressions
                |> withInteriorNewlines (styleOf original)
                |> endedLike original.expressions
    }


withInteriorNewlines : Newline -> Array Expression -> Array Expression
withInteriorNewlines style expressions =
    let
        lastIndex =
            Array.length expressions - 1
    in
    Array.indexedMap
        (\index expression ->
            if index == lastIndex || expression.newline /= Nothing then
                expression

            else
                { expression | newline = Just style }
        )
        expressions


{-| Give the result the same ending the original had.
-}
endedLike : Array Expression -> Array Expression -> Array Expression
endedLike original result =
    when { was = lastNewline original, now = lastNewline result } is
        { was = Nothing, now = Just style } ->
            setLastNewline Nothing result

        { was = Just style, now = Nothing } ->
            setLastNewline (Just style) result

        _ ->
            result


lastNewline : Array Expression -> Maybe Newline
lastNewline expressions =
    Array.last expressions
        |> Maybe.andThen .newline


setLastNewline : Maybe Newline -> Array Expression -> Array Expression
setLastNewline ending expressions =
    when Array.popLast expressions is
        Just { last, initial } ->
            Array.pushLast { last | newline = ending } initial

        Nothing ->
            expressions


-- FINDING


{-| The index of the expression holding the key at `path`.

The *last* one, if there are several. In a document that means anything there
are several only when the path runs through an array of tables, and then the
last item is the one TOML itself means by the path -- see [what a path means
here](#what-a-path-means-here). It is also the item a new key is added to, so
reading and writing agree about which `[[peer]]` a path names.

-}
indexOf : Array String -> Document -> Maybe Int
indexOf path document =
    Array.map2
        (\expression header ->
            when expression.content is
                Pair fields ->
                    Array.prepend header (Literal.path fields.keyval.key) == path

                _ ->
                    False
        )
        document.expressions
        (headers document.expressions)
        |> lastIndexOfTrue


lastIndexOfTrue : Array Bool -> Maybe Int
lastIndexOfTrue flags =
    Array.indexedFoldl
        (\index flag found ->
            if flag then
                Just index

            else
                found
        )
        Nothing
        flags


{-| The index of the `[header]` line for the table at `path`.

The *first* one, if there are several, which for an array of tables is its
first `[[item]]`. That is the opposite choice from [`indexOf`](#indexOf), and it
is deliberate rather than an oversight: a path into an array of tables means
the last item when it names a value, because that is the item TOML means, but
the comment block over a header explains the key -- the whole array -- and the
first header is where `Toml.Encode` writes it. The two live in separate
functions so that neither choice can leak into the other.

-}
headerIndexOf : Array String -> Document -> Maybe Int
headerIndexOf path document =
    Array.indexedFoldl
        (\index expression found ->
            when { found = found, content = expression.content } is
                { found = Nothing, content = Header fields } ->
                    if Literal.path fields.header.key == path then
                        Just index

                    else
                        Nothing

                _ ->
                    found
        )
        Nothing
        document.expressions


{-| The table header in effect at each expression, in file order.

For a header expression this is its own path, so that `[a]` counts as being in
`a` -- which is what makes a key inserted into `a` land after the header rather
than before it.

-}
headers : Array Expression -> Array (Array String)
headers expressions =
    Array.foldl
        (\expression soFar ->
            let
                next =
                    when expression.content is
                        Header fields ->
                            Literal.path fields.header.key

                        _ ->
                            soFar.current
            in
            { current = next, found = Array.pushLast next soFar.found }
        )
        { current = [], found = [] }
        expressions
        |> .found


{-| The first expression that belongs to the key at `index`: the start of the
comment block directly above it, or the key itself if there is none.

A blank line ends the block, and so does anything that is not an own-line
comment.

-}
firstOwned : Int -> Array Expression -> Int
firstOwned index expressions =
    let
        walk at =
            if at <= 0 then
                at

            else
                when Array.get (at - 1) expressions is
                    Just previous ->
                        when previous.content is
                            Empty fields ->
                                when fields.comment is
                                    Just _ ->
                                        walk (at - 1)

                                    Nothing ->
                                        at

                            _ ->
                                at

                    Nothing ->
                        at
    in
    walk index


cut : Int -> Int -> Array Expression -> Array Expression
cut from to expressions =
    Array.prepend
        (Array.takeFirst from expressions)
        (Array.dropFirst to expressions)


-- INSERTING


insert : Array String -> Value -> Document -> Document
insert path content document =
    let
        tablePath =
            Array.dropLast 1 path

        leaf =
            Maybe.withDefault "" (Array.last path)

        pair =
            { key = Literal.key [ leaf ]
            , wsBeforeEq = " "
            , wsAfterEq = " "
            , value = content
            }
    in
    when { last = lastOf tablePath document, isRoot = Array.length tablePath == 0 } is
        { last = Just index } ->
            insertAfter index pair document

        { last = Nothing, isRoot = True } ->
            -- No top-level key to go after. Anything appended now would land
            -- inside the last table, so the key goes ahead of the first header
            -- instead, if there is one.
            when firstHeader document is
                Just index ->
                    insertBefore index pair document

                Nothing ->
                    append tablePath pair document

        _ ->
            append tablePath pair document


{-| The first `[header]` in the file, which is where the top-level keys end.
-}
firstHeader : Document -> Maybe Int
firstHeader document =
    Array.indexedFoldl
        (\index expression found ->
            when { found = found, content = expression.content } is
                { found = Nothing, content = Header _ } ->
                    Just index

                _ ->
                    found
        )
        Nothing
        document.expressions


{-| A top-level key ahead of the first header, above the comment block that
belongs to the header, with a blank line between the key and what it was put
in front of. The blank line is the same one [`Toml.Encode`](Toml-Encode) puts
before a header, and here it keeps the new key out of the header's block.
-}
insertBefore : Int -> Ast.KeyVal -> Document -> Document
insertBefore index pair document =
    let
        at =
            firstOwned index document.expressions

        style =
            styleOf document

        added =
            [ { indent = ""
              , content = Pair { keyval = pair, trailing = "", comment = Nothing }
              , newline = Just style
              }
            , { indent = "", content = Empty { comment = Nothing }, newline = Just style }
            ]
    in
    rebuild document
        (Array.prepend
            (Array.takeFirst at document.expressions)
            (Array.prepend added (Array.dropFirst at document.expressions))
        )


{-| The last header or key belonging to `tablePath`, which is where a new key
goes. Trailing blank lines and floating comments are deliberately skipped: a new
key belongs beside the keys, not after the gap. For an array of tables this is
in the last item, which is the item [`indexOf`](#indexOf) finds too.
-}
lastOf : Array String -> Document -> Maybe Int
lastOf tablePath document =
    Array.map2
        (\expression header ->
            header == tablePath
                && (when expression.content is
                        Empty _ ->
                            False

                        _ ->
                            True
                   )
        )
        document.expressions
        (headers document.expressions)
        |> Array.indexedFoldl
            (\index flag found ->
                if flag then
                    Just index

                else
                    found
            )
            Nothing


insertAfter : Int -> Ast.KeyVal -> Document -> Document
insertAfter index pair document =
    when Array.get index document.expressions is
        Nothing ->
            document

        Just neighbour ->
            rebuild document
                (Array.insert (index + 1)
                    { indent = neighbour.indent
                    , content = Pair { keyval = pair, trailing = "", comment = Nothing }
                    , newline = Just (styleOf document)
                    }
                    document.expressions
                )


{-| No table for this key yet, so the file grows a `[header]` and the key under
it. Both go at the end of what the file already says, with a blank line above
the header the way [`Toml.Encode`](Toml-Encode) writes one -- unless the file is
empty, where there is nothing above to be separated from.
-}
append : Array String -> Ast.KeyVal -> Document -> Document
append tablePath pair document =
    let
        style =
            styleOf document

        keyLine =
            { indent = ""
            , content = Pair { keyval = pair, trailing = "", comment = Nothing }
            , newline = Just style
            }

        headerLine =
            { indent = ""
            , content =
                Header
                    { header =
                        { kind = Std
                        , wsBefore = ""
                        , key = Literal.key tablePath
                        , wsAfter = ""
                        }
                    , trailing = ""
                    , comment = Nothing
                    }
            , newline = Just style
            }

        separator =
            if saysAnything document.expressions then
                [ { indent = "", content = Empty { comment = Nothing }, newline = Just style } ]

            else
                []

        added =
            if Array.length tablePath == 0 then
                [ keyLine ]

            else
                Array.prepend separator [ headerLine, keyLine ]
    in
    rebuild document (beforeTerminator added document.expressions)


{-| Put new expressions at the end of the file, but *before* the empty
expression that sits after the final newline when there is one.

That expression is how the AST says the file ended with a newline: it holds
nothing and has no newline of its own, and being last is the whole of its job.
Appending past it would make it an interior expression, which `rebuild` would
then give a newline of its own -- and the file, which used to end with one,
would not any more.

-}
beforeTerminator : Array Expression -> Array Expression -> Array Expression
beforeTerminator added expressions =
    when Array.popLast expressions is
        Just { last, initial } ->
            if isTerminator last then
                Array.pushLast last (Array.prepend initial added)

            else
                Array.prepend expressions added

        Nothing ->
            added


{-| Whether this is the empty expression a file that ends with a newline ends
with. A comment with no newline after it is a line of the file and is not one.
-}
isTerminator : Expression -> Bool
isTerminator expression =
    when expression.content is
        Empty fields ->
            fields.comment == Nothing && expression.newline == Nothing

        _ ->
            False


{-| Whether the file has anything in it but blank lines, which is what decides
if an appended section needs a blank line above it.
-}
saysAnything : Array Expression -> Bool
saysAnything expressions =
    Array.any
        (\expression ->
            when expression.content is
                Empty fields ->
                    fields.comment /= Nothing

                _ ->
                    True
        )
        expressions


{-| Which line ending this file uses, so that a line added to it matches. The
first one wins; a file that mixes them is already odd and this does not make it
odder.
-}
styleOf : Document -> Newline
styleOf document =
    Array.mapAndKeepJust .newline document.expressions
        |> Array.first
        |> Maybe.withDefault Lf


-- VALUES


{-| A string, written as a basic one: `"..."` on one line.
-}
string : String -> Value
string =
    Literal.string


{-| A string, written as a multi-line one: `"""..."""`, with the newlines in it
written as themselves rather than as `\n`.

The form for a value a person is meant to read: a note, a key, a query. Every
string can be written this way, so this never fails.

**Setting a value the file already means changes nothing**, spelling included --
see [`set`](#set). A key that already says this in a basic string keeps the
basic string; the form is chosen when the key is first written.

-}
multilineString : String -> Value
multilineString =
    Literal.multilineString


{-| A string, written as a literal one: `'...'`, with no escapes at all.

`Nothing` when the string has an apostrophe, a newline, or a control character
other than tab in it, since a literal string has no way to spell those. It does
not fall back to another form: which form a key is written in should not depend
on what the value happens to contain.

-}
literalString : String -> Maybe Value
literalString =
    Literal.literalString


{-| A string, written as a multi-line literal one: `'''...'''`: no escapes, and
the newlines written as themselves.

`Nothing` when the string cannot be spelled that way -- three apostrophes in a
row, an apostrophe at the very end, a lone carriage return, or a control
character other than tab and newline.

-}
multilineLiteralString : String -> Maybe Value
multilineLiteralString =
    Literal.multilineLiteralString


{-| An integer that fits in a Gren `Int`.
-}
int : Int -> Value
int =
    Literal.int


{-| An integer of any size.
-}
bigInt : BigInt -> Value
bigInt =
    Literal.bigInt


{-| A float. `inf` and `nan` are written as those words; everything else gets a
decimal point even when it does not need one, since `1` without one is an
integer in TOML.
-}
float : Float -> Value
float =
    Literal.float


{-| An exact decimal.
-}
bigDecimal : BigDecimal -> Value
bigDecimal =
    Literal.bigDecimal


{-| A boolean.
-}
bool : Bool -> Value
bool =
    Literal.bool


{-| A date and time with an offset.
-}
offsetDateTime : DateTime -> Value
offsetDateTime =
    Literal.offsetDateTime


{-| A date and time with no offset.
-}
localDateTime : DateTime -> Value
localDateTime =
    Literal.localDateTime


{-| A date.
-}
localDate : Date -> Value
localDate =
    Literal.localDate


{-| A time of day.
-}
localTime : Time -> Value
localTime =
    Literal.localTime


{-| An array, on one line: `[1, 2, 3]`.

    Toml.Edit.set [ "ports" ]
        (Toml.Edit.array [ Toml.Edit.int 80, Toml.Edit.int 443 ])

An array already in the file keeps every character of itself for as long as
nothing is set over it, newlines and comments inside the brackets included.
This is the spelling for one that is being written fresh.

-}
array : Array Value -> Value
array =
    Literal.array


{-| An inline table, on one line: `{ a = 1, b = 2 }`.

    Toml.Edit.set [ "limits" ]
        (Toml.Edit.inlineTable
            [ { key = "soft", value = Toml.Edit.int 1024 }
            , { key = "hard", value = Toml.Edit.int 4096 }
            ]
        )

This is also how to change something *inside* an inline table, since a path does
not reach in -- see [what a path means](#what-a-path-means-here). Build the table
you want and set the whole of it.

There is no builder here for a `[header]` table, because a header is a line
rather than a value and `set` puts values on lines. A key under a header that
does not exist yet already brings one with it.

-}
inlineTable : Array { key : String, value : Value } -> Value
inlineTable =
    Literal.inlineTable
